PEDOT:PSS (Clevios P VP AI 4083) layer was deposited by spin coating at 3000 rpm for 45 s and then annealing at 120 $^{\circ}C$ for 10 min. Finally, a $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick silver layer was deposited by thermal evaporation at a base pressure of $2\times{\bf10}^{-6}$ mbar.
Device characterization. The current-voltage curves of solar cells were measured inside the glovebox using a Keithley 2400 source-measure unit. The cells were illuminated by a 450 W Class AAA solar simulator equipped with an AM1. $_{5}\mathrm{G}$ filter (Sol3A, Oriel Instruments) at a calibrated intensity of 100 $\mathrm{\mW/cm}^{2}$ , as determined by a standard silicon reference cell (91150V Oriel Instruments). The effective area of the cell was defined to be $\mathbf{o.o71cm^{2}}$ using a non-reflective metal mask. IPCE spectra were measured in air using a commercial IPCE setup (QE-PVSi, Oriel Instruments). Monochromated light was chopped at a frequency of 30 $\mathrm{Hz}$ and photocurrents measured using a lock-in amplifier. Impedance spectroscopy measurements were carried out by Solartron SI1260 Impedance/Gain-phase analyzer, under dark and illumination conditions by applying a $5~\mathrm{mV}$ voltage perturbation with the frequency ranging between 400 kHz and 0.1 Hz. SEM images were acquired on a Hitachi $S_{-4}800$ field emission microscope. AFM measurements were carried out using a Dimension Hybrid (Bruker/Veeco Metrology) Atomic Force Microscope operating in contact mode in air. Absorbance spectra were acquired on a Cary 6000i spectrophotometer.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Photovoltaic performance with and without the electron transfer layer. n-type metal oxides such as titanium oxide $\mathrm{(TiO_{x})}$ and zinc oxide $(\mathrm{ZnO})$ have been widely used in polymer solar cells32,33 due to their transparency and high electron mobility. These same materials have also found ubiquitous use as the electrontransporting (hole-blocking) layer in perovskite solar cells;1-4 even in devices that use a mesoporous (and insulating) $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{}^{27}$ or $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}^{34}$ scaffold, a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer is typically first deposited on the transparent electrode. Since analogous devices lacking a hole-transport layer have already been fabricated and have been shown to perform well,20-24 here we prepared planar heterojunction perovskite solar cells via a two-step deposition process7,35 in two different architectures – both with and without a $\mathrm{znO}$ ETL (Figure 1) – in order to determine how the ETL influences cell performance. To compare these results to those of excitonic solar cells, we also fabricated and tested inverted bulk heterojunction cells based on poly $\dot{3}$ -hexylthiophene) $(\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT})$ / [6,6]-phenyl- $C_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $\mathrm{(PC_{61}B M)}$ active layers in both the $\mathrm{ITO/ZnO/P_{3}H T:P C_{61}B M/P E D O T/A g}$ and $\mathrm{ITO/P_{3}H T:P C_{61}B M/P E D O T/A g}$ architectures.
The device architectures and an energy level diagram of the components are shown in Figure 1. ITO-coated float glass was either first coated with a layer of $\mathrm{znO}$ nanoparticles or used as-is; perovskite films were then deposited on top via the sequential deposition method.7,35 Top-view and cross-sectional SEM images of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film are shown in Figure 1c, which highlight the relatively large crystallite size (ca. $\mathbf{150}\ \mathrm{nm},$ ) and excellent surface coverage produced by the two-step deposition technique. A $\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT}$ layer was then spin coated on top of the perovskite as the hole transport material, and a thermally evaporated silver film completed the device structure. The current-voltage $\left(J{-}V\right)$ curves of the best devices are shown in Figure 2a. The best cell based on the $\mathrm{ITO/ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P_{3}H T/A g}$ structure delivered a short-circuit current density of $18.8~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.94 V and a fill factor (FF) of $66.0\%$ , leading to a PCE of $11.7\%$ under standard $\mathrm{AM}_{1.5}\mathrm{G}$ illumination. However, even without the ZnO ETL, the device performance is still highly competitive with this benchmark. The best $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}}$ free device displayed a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $\mathrm{17.2\mA/cm^{2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.01 V and a FF of $66.5\%$ , which yielded a PCE of $11.6\%$ . These numbers are in good agreement with the IPCE spectra. The onset of photocurrent at $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ is consistent with the optical bandgap of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3},{^{36}}$ and the IPCE spectrum effectively covers the entire visible region. The relatively flat spectral profile and high maximum efficiency (\~ $75\%$ ) are indicative of excellent light harvesting efficiency. Integration of the product of the IPCE spectrum and the $\mathrm{AM}_{1.5}\mathrm{G}$ solar photon flux yields a projected current density of $\scriptstyle17.1{\mathrm{~mA}}/{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ , which is in excellent agreement with the experimentally-measured $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . Any differences between the simulated sunlight and the AM1.5G standard are therefore assumed to be negligible. The results were statistically validated by testing a batch of $76$ separate ZnOfree devices. The average device performance parameters are summarized in Table S1, alongside those of $\mathrm{znO}$ -based control devices. The average $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and fill factor are very slightly lower for the $\mathrm{znO}$ -free devices than for the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}}$ based controls; however, this is offset by a slightly higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The net result is that the overall power conversion efficiency of the $\mathrm{znO}$ -free devices is essentially indistinguishable from that of the cells prepared using a $\mathrm{{znO}}$ layer.
Additionally, since $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di(4- methoxyphenyl)amino)- ${\bf\cdot9},{\bf9^{'}}$ -spirobifluorene (SpiroOMeTAD) has been shown to be a more effective hole transport material than $\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT}$ , we also fabricated $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}}$ free devices using a Spiro-OMeTAD HTL. The $\mathrm{znO}$ -free $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}/$ Spiro-OMeTAD devices displayed PCEs of up to $13.5\%$ , with an average value of $11.6\%$ (Figure 2d). This compares very favorably with control devices containing a $\mathrm{znO}$ ETL, which were previously reported to have an average PCE of $13{\cdot}7\%$ .7 It is worth noting that in both the $\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT}$ and Spiro-OMeTAD devices, the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is still $\sim\bf{\tau}_{1}\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ (Figure 2c). Clearly, the lack of a ETL does not result in extensive charge carrier recombination, which would be expected to substantially decrease the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . In marked contrast, the polymer $(\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT}{:}\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM})$ solar cells show very different performance after removal of the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ layer (Figure 2b and Table S1). The highest performing $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}}$ based control device had a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $9.81\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.58 V and a FF of $64.2\%$ , yielding a PCE of $3.62\%$ . After the
$\mathrm{{znO}}$ layer was removed, the PCE of the best device was reduced to $1.29\%$ , only one third of that of the corresponding control device. While the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $8.16\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ represents only a modest decrease from the maximum of $9.81~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.40 $\mathrm{v}$ and FF of $39.6\%$ were dramatically lower. Here the $\mathrm{znO}$ ETL is absolutely essential to proper device function; without it, charge recombination at the polymer/ITO interface leads to a significant decrease in both $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF.
Photoluminescence and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy. While the collapse in the performance of the $\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT:PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ system upon removal of the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ ETL is to be expected, the anomalously high performance of the analogous $\mathrm{znO}$ -free perovskite cell is not. The substantial energy difference between the bottom of the perovskite conduction band and the ITO Fermi energy (0.7 eV) would ordinarily be expected to hinder efficient electron transfer across the perovskite/ITO interface; however, clearly that does not appear to be a limiting factor here. In order to better understand the high performance of the $\mathrm{znO}$ -free devices, photoluminescence spectroscopy was used to compare the relative efficiency of electron- and hole-extraction from the perovskite.
Figure 3a shows the photoluminescence spectrum of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film in a variety of bilayer configurations, including Glass/ $\mathrm{{CH}}_{3}\mathrm{{NH}}_{3}\mathrm{{PbI}}_{3}$ , Glass/ITO $\mathrm{'CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}.$
$\mathrm{Glass/ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}},$ , $\mathrm{Glass/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P_{3}H T}$ and Glass $/C\mathrm{H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/S p i r o-O M e T A D.}$ The photoluminescence peak at $760~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is entirely consistent with previous reports of emission from $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3},{^{37}}$ and the spectral position of the emission is consistent among all of the samples. However, from the intensity of the peak, it can be seen that there is a substantial quenching effect when the perovskite is placed in contact with either ITO or the HTL/ETL materials. Emission from the perovskite was almost entirely quenched upon contact with the two hole transport materials $\mathrm{\Phi{P3HT}}$ and Spiro-OMeTAD); however, only $\sim40\%$ photoluminescence quenching was observed when the perovskite was deposited on either $\mathrm{znO}$ or ITO. This is consistent with carrier extraction at the perovskite/HTL interface being more efficient than at the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ or ITO/perovskite interface; given the high surface area of the perovskite/HTL interface $(R_{\mathrm{rms}}\:=\:65\:\mathrm{\:nm}$ , Figure S1), and the more planar $\mathrm{znO}$ and ITO surfaces ( $\mathrm{\hat{\rho}}R_{\mathrm{rms}}={\bf{1}}3$ and $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively), this is likely at least partially driven by morphological differences. Highly efficient hole extraction at the perovskite/HTL interface would at least partially explain the high performance of the ZnO-free devices; by rapidly depleting the perovskite of holes, bimolecular recombination would be at least partially suppressed. It is also important to note that the degree of photoluminescence quenching in the Glass $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}Z n O/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and
Glass/ $\mathrm{ITO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ samples is very similar. Since the $R_{\mathrm{rms}}$ values of both the $\mathrm{znO}$ and ITO surfaces are not dramatically different (both form essentially flat interfaces with the perovskite), and both produce similar levels of photoluminescence quenching, it can be concluded that they have similar rates of electron transfer at the interface.
In order to better understand charge transport and interfacial charge transfer processes, we carried out electrochemical impedance spectroscopy on both $\mathrm{ITO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P_{3}H T/A g}$ and $\mathrm{ITO/ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P_{3}H T/A g}$ devices (Figure 4). EIS is a powerful characterization technique for studying the interfacial charge transfer properties of photovoltaic devices,38 and has recently been used to investigate the carrier transport and recombination dynamics in mesostructured perovskite solar cells.39-43 Figure 4a shows the Nyquist plots of devices both with and without $\mathrm{znO}$ electron-transport layers, as measured at the open circuit potential under 1 sun $\mathrm{AM}_{1.5}\mathrm{G}$ illumination. Nyquist plots of the same devices in the dark are shown in Figure S2. The data in the Nyquist plot is clearly separated into two distinct $R C$ arcs; although recent reports have occasionally revealed the presence of a third feature at low frequencies,39,40 we see no evidence for it in the present case. As such, we have fit the data to a relatively simple equivalent circuit (Figure 4b) consisting of a parallel $R C$ and $R–C P E$ element connected in series, along with an additional contribution from series resistance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ . Although more elaborate models have been used to describe both perovskite and solid-state dye-sensitized solar cells in the past, $4^{0-42}$ we have elected to use the simplest model capable of producing a good fit to the experimental data. The model is essentially identical to that used by Boix et al. in their analysis of solid-state $\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ photovoltaic devices,44 where the high frequency $R C$ element is ascribed to contact resistance at either the ETL/absorber or absorber/HTL interface, while the lower frequency element is associated with the recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ and chemical capacitance $(C_{\upmu})$ of the system.44,45 In comparing the Nyquist plots for the devices with and without the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ layer, it can be seen that both arcs have been substantially affected; as such, we ascribe the higher frequency feature to the contact impedance associated with the ITO/perovskite or $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO}}/$ perovskite interface (since the perovskite $/\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{HT}$ interface is identical in both cases).
The data were then fit to the equivalent circuit shown in Figure 4b, and the values of $R_{s},$ $R_{\mathrm{co}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ are tabulated in Table S2. As expected, the devices that include a $\mathrm{znO}$ ETL have a reduced contact resistance, in keeping with the more favorable energy level alignment of the $\mathrm{znO}$ and perovskite conduction bands. This allows for more efficient extraction of the electrons at the $\mathrm{ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface, and is consistent with the slightly greater photoluminescence quenching observed for the Glass $\mathrm{\Delta/ZnO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ sample as opposed to the ITO analogue (Figure 3). However, from the perspective of overall device performance, this reduction in contact resistance is offset by a concomitant decrease in the recombination resistance. ZnO is known to provide high surface energy sites that can lead to substantial surface recombination; previous work has shown that high surface area $\mathrm{znO}$ nanostructures lead to relatively poor device perfor
